{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# The `(inbound, probe)` dispatch chain is a documented subsystem with no live producer\n\n## Status\n\nDISCOVERED while fixing d103-c3f8-2fbc-4c97 / 727f-b351-59ba-4b3b. Not a regression those\nfixes introduced - it predates them and neither changes it. Filed so it is not lost.\n\n## What is unreachable\n\n`differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes` is the only producer of\n`(inbound, probe)` mutations. It iterates `local_state` looking for entries carrying a\nbound `jira_key` field. Its sole production caller, `run_differs`, passes\n`prev_snapshot` - a FETCHED Jira snapshot, whose entries never carry `jira_key`\n(`fetcher.py`, and the contract shape in `_snapshot_schema.py`). The loop therefore never\nemits, and everything downstream of it is dead in production:\n\n* `_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch` in `run_differs.py`\n* `inbound_probe.py` (`ProbeResult`, `ProbeBranch`)\n* the router `route_inbound_probe` — which lives in `reconcile_helpers.py` and is re-exported at\n  `reconcile.py:66`, NOT in `inbound_probe.py` as an earlier draft of this section claimed\n* `backend.probe_remote` / the `SupportsAbsenceProbe` capability\n\nConfirmed empirically: fed a realistic fetched-snapshot pair, `compute_mutations` emits only\n`jira_new`, `unbound_local` and `field_drift`. The only fixture in the repo that supplies a\n`jira_key`-bearing `local_state` is\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py`.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThis is the mechanism ADR 0028 para 2 relies on to tell \"deleted\" from \"aged out of the fetch\nwindow\" - deletion is proven only by a bounded direct GET returning 404. With no live producer,\nthat discrimination is never exercised in production.\n\nThe live DC cell that once asserted the probe WAS re-oracled, but the inherited wording for it was\nwrong and is corrected here (see CITATION-3B5F-D). The rename\n`test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe` ->\n`test_a_deleted_dc_issue_never_plans_a_local_teardown` landed in commit `ae009443a9`, whose subject is\n`b9b8: mirror a Jira-side title edit inbound`. It was BUNDLED INTO b9b8's commit, not part of b9b8's\nscope (b9b8 is a title-mirroring bug in `outbound_field_diff.py:38-45`). The cell today asserts the\nADR 0028 para 1 observable and PASSED live in harness run 30933704255.\n\n## What is NOT the fix\n\nDo NOT re-enable the arm by making `run_differs` pass a `jira_key`-bearing local_state without\nalso removing `reconcile_helpers.drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` in the SAME change.\nThat suppression exists only because the caller passes the legacy `(prev, curr)` pair; migrating\nthe caller makes the suppressed emissions correct again, and leaving both in place would silently\ndrop real work.\n\n## OPERATOR RULING (2026-08-04): once an issue is deleted in Jira, it shouldn't be resurrected.\n\nThis settles the question DECISION-3B5F-C escalated, and it widens the ticket: rebar resurrects TODAY,\non a delay, through a path no ticket has examined.\n\n**Measured, not assumed** (real `BindingStore`): 3 consecutive confirmed-404 `note_absent` calls retire\nthe binding; `_retire` (`binding_store.py:564-577`) pops the entry from BOTH `bindings` and `reverse`;\n`get_jira_key` then returns `None`, so the local ticket is UNBOUND; and `outbound_differ.py:502-513`\nemits an outbound CREATE for any unbound local ticket. A fresh Jira issue therefore replaces the\ndeliberately-deleted one about 3 passes later.\n\nPrior art agrees with the ruling: Unito will not recreate deleted work items, \"assuming that once a\nwork item is deleted, you don't want it back\", explicitly to stop flows re-creating things you wanted\ngone; Kubernetes reconcilers treat a missing object with `IgnoreNotFound` and never resurrect.\n\n**`is_retired` does not prevent this.** It exists (`binding_store.py:612`) and is consulted at\n`outbound_differ.py:590`, but only to keep retired keys OUT of the GET-probe candidate list. It is\nkeyed by `jira_key`, and the unbound-create arm has no retired check at all.\n\n## Plan\n\n**A0. Define the escape hatch - it does not exist yet.** There is NO un-retire API: `is_retired` reads\n(`binding_store.py:612`), `clear_absent` only resets the 404 counter (`:589`), and `_retire` (`:564`) is\nprivate and one-way. A tombstone would be permanent with no route out but hand-editing\n`.bridge_state/bindings-retired.json`. So this adds `BindingStore.unretire(jira_key)`: drop the key\nfrom the retired set and file so the next pass treats the ticket as ordinary unbound work. The\nsuppression message names this call.\n\n**A. Tombstone a confirmed-deleted pairing so it is never re-created.**\n1. Add `BindingStore.is_retired_local(local_id) -> bool` - a reverse lookup over the retired entries\n   (`bindings-retired.json` is keyed by `jira_key` and carries `local_id`, so only the accessor is\n   missing). Same duck-typed/fail-open tolerance as `outbound_differ._is_retired`.\n2. Guard the unbound-create arm at `outbound_differ.py:510-513`: a local ticket with NO live binding\n   but WITH a retired one was previously paired with a remote that returned a confirmed 404 - skip the\n   create. A never-bound ticket still creates normally; that distinction is the whole point.\n3. Make it VISIBLE: a bridge alert / sync-log line naming the local id and the retired key, and naming\n   `unretire` as the route back.\n\n**B. Delete the dead legacy probe arm:** the producer\n`differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`, the dispatch arm\n`_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, `route_inbound_probe` (`reconcile_helpers.py`) and its\nre-export at `reconcile.py:66`.\n\n**B2. THE CONSUMER INVENTORY — every module that exercises a removed symbol.** A prior revision of\nthis plan named only the fixture-only test, which under-declared the blast radius: eight further\nmodules reference these symbols directly and would break unaddressed. `review-plan` blocked on\nexactly that, correctly. The complete set, and the disposition of each:\n\n*Deleted* (the module's whole subject is the removed chain):\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py` — the only fixture supplying a\n  `jira_key`-bearing `local_state`, i.e. a synthetic input the real caller never produces\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_inbound_probe_dispatch.py` — exercises\n  `route_inbound_probe` across all branches\n\n*Updated* (the module has a legitimate wider subject; only the dead-chain assertions go, and a\ncomment records the removal so the next reader is not left guessing):\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_probe_capability.py` — called\n  `_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch` directly\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/fetch/test_probe_capability_heldout.py` — likewise\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_snapshot_differ_stale_local_state.py` — asserted the\n  dispatch as a stubbed phase\n\nSource modules carrying the removals besides the four named above: `reconcile.py` (re-export),\n`typed_dispatch.py`, `applier.py`, `apply_inbound.py`, `_backend.py`, `inbound_probe.py` (dormancy\nnote only).\n\n**B1. SCOPE BOUNDARY - the port capability stays, as a reasoned exclusion.** `inbound_probe.py`,\n`backend.probe_remote` and `SupportsAbsenceProbe` also lose their only consumer, but removing DC's\n`probe_remote` (`adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py:213-229`) removes DC's only import of the shared\n`classify_probe_response`, leaving it used by Cloud alone (`adapters/jira/probe.py:85,98,100`) - which\nweakens **epic e369 AC5**, whose claim is that the probe classifier has one implementation in\n`jira_family` imported by BOTH backends. Trading an epic-level AC to delete a dormant Protocol is a\nseparate decision; recorded as dormant with a follow-up.\n\n**C. Do NOT restore same-pass re-create - and REMOVE its implementation rather than orphaning it.**\nAn earlier draft kept `_apply_inbound_delete` \"for a possible re-wire\"; `review-plan` blocked that as\nleaving a removed producer's consumer behind, and under the ruling the objection is stronger than the\nreviewer put it: `create_after_hard_delete` (`apply_inbound.py:220`) is not merely unreachable, it is\nnow UNWANTED - it IS the resurrection ruled against. So the leaf goes: `_apply_inbound_delete`\n(`apply_inbound.py:175-284`), its `(\"inbound\",\"delete\")` entry (`typed_dispatch.py:92`), the\n`_apply_inbound_probe` marker leaf and its `applier.py` registration, and the\n`create_after_hard_delete` consumption at `applier.py:452`. No half-wired resurrection path remains.\n\n**C1. Plan C's consumer inventory.** Removing the leaf orphans its builders, so they go too:\n`_build_hard_delete_recreate` and the `pending_hard_delete_creates` plumbing, plus\n`reconcile_helpers._audit_log_probe`. Test consumers:\n\n*Deleted*: `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_hard_delete_recreate.py` — its entire subject is\nthe same-pass re-create this ruling forbids.\n\n*Updated*: `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/test_inbound_leaf_bodies.py` (asserted\n`create_after_hard_delete`) and `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_leaves_effect_matrix.py`\n(asserted the `(inbound,probe)` marker leaf via `_drive_inbound_probe`).\n\n*Deliberately NOT modified*: `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_applier.py` and\n`apply/test_applier_direction_guard.py` iterate the `_LEAVES` registry and cross-check it against\n`mutation._VALID_COMBINATIONS`. They keep passing unmodified because they assert\n`_LEAVES` ⊆ `_VALID_COMBINATIONS`, not the converse. **Known loose end, stated rather than hidden:**\n`_VALID_COMBINATIONS` still permits `(inbound, delete)` and `(inbound, probe)`, so such a mutation\nwould now raise `UnknownActionError` at dispatch rather than being rejected up front. Narrowing the\nallowlist is deliberately out of scope here — it would change a shared validation contract this\nticket did not review — and it is recorded so a reviewer agrees to it rather than discovers it.\n\n**D. `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` STAYS.** The caller is unchanged, so the suppression\nis exactly as correct as today. (The \"what is NOT the fix\" trap applies to the WIRE option, not taken.)\n\n**E. Correct ADR 0028.** Its Consequences assert c244 re-creates a hard-deleted issue in the same pass.\nThat is false today and, under this ruling, no longer desired. Replace with tombstone semantics and\nnote that absence-vs-deletion discrimination lives in `outbound_differ` + `binding_walk`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Establish whether the absence-probe path is still wanted, or whether ADR 0028 para 2's\n      direct-GET confirmation has since been implemented elsewhere. **ANSWERED** — implemented twice\n      and both live: `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue` (L13) and `binding_walk.py:187-196` (ADR para 4),\n      called at `run_differs.py:222` / `:257`. Verified live on DC: Row 14\n      `test_a_deleted_dc_issue_never_plans_a_local_teardown` PASSED in harness run 30933704255.\n- [x] ~~a RED-first test proving `(inbound, probe)` is produced from the real production call path~~ —\n      **MOOT** under the operator ruling; no producer is to be created.\n- [ ] the dead chain is removed: `differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`,\n      `_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, `route_inbound_probe`, and the fixture-only\n      `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/diffing/test_differ_emits_probe.py`\n- [ ] RED-first: a local ticket whose binding was retired on a confirmed 404 gets NO outbound create —\n      the test FAILS before the guard exists and PASSES after, checked by running it at both commits\n- [ ] a never-bound local ticket STILL gets its outbound create, asserted in the same test module so\n      the guard cannot suppress ordinary creation\n- [ ] the suppression is observable (bridge alert or sync-log line) naming the local id and the retired\n      key, and the message names `BindingStore.unretire(<jira_key>)` as the route to re-enable creation\n- [ ] `BindingStore.unretire(jira_key)` exists, drops the key from BOTH the in-memory retired set and\n      `bindings-retired.json`, and a test proves the local ticket is created again on the next pass —\n      so the tombstone is reversible by a documented call rather than by hand-editing state\n- [ ] the dormant port capability (`probe_remote` / `SupportsAbsenceProbe` / `inbound_probe.py`) is\n      recorded as dormant with a follow-up ticket, and the reason it is not removed here (epic e369\n      AC5's both-backends claim on `classify_probe_response`) is stated\n- [ ] `BindingStore.is_retired_local(local_id)` tolerates legacy/duck-typed stores the way\n      `outbound_differ._is_retired` does — asserted with a stub store lacking the method\n- [ ] the resurrection implementation is removed, not orphaned: `_apply_inbound_delete`, the\n      `(\"inbound\",\"delete\")` typed_dispatch entry, the `_apply_inbound_probe` marker leaf, and the\n      `create_after_hard_delete` consumption in `applier.py` — verified by the suite passing with them\n      gone and a grep showing no remaining reference\n- [ ] ADR 0028's c244 Consequence is corrected to describe tombstone semantics\n- [ ] `drop_snapshot_differ_local_state_emissions` is still present and still consulted — asserted, so\n      the WIRE trap cannot be entered by accident\n\n## Testing\n\n    pytest tests/unit/rebar_reconciler -q                    # the reconciler tier\n    pytest tests/unit -q                                     # full unit suite (no regressions)\n    make lint && make typecheck\n\nThe RED-first proof for the tombstone guard is run explicitly at two commits: the test is added\nfirst and must FAIL against the pre-guard tree, then PASS once the guard lands. A guard asserted\nwithout a demonstrated RED is unverified, and this one suppresses work — the failure mode of a\nwrong guard is silently not creating issues, which no green suite would surface.\n\nRemoval of the dead chain is proved by the suite continuing to pass with the producer, the dispatch\narm and the router gone — plus a grep confirming no remaining `(inbound, probe)` producer, since the\ndynamic loader means an import-graph check alone would miss a string-loaded module."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785880295561078001,"uuid":"dcf5c6dd-e76d-444a-a97b-b41fd4540444"}